window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][73] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Time spent per user on your social app is up 8% after a ranking change. Is that good?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, more time spent is more engagement.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what changed, decomposes the increase, checks quality and counter metrics, and gives a conditional answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Challenges time spent as a proxy for value, separates active from passive time, checks retention and well-being signals, considers the second-order effects, and says what would make it good.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Setting only: no company data is used.</em> I'd say &quot;it depends on where the time comes from,&quot; and then find out. First, clarify the metric: is it total minutes per daily user, and how is time measured (foreground time, or including time with the app idle)? What exactly changed in ranking, and when? Second, decompose. Time per user = sessions per user × minutes per session. And split the time by kind: interacting with people (commenting, messaging) versus passive consumption (scrolling, watching). If the 8% is almost all passive video, it is a different story from an 8% rise in conversations. Third, check quality. Look at next-day and 30-day retention of the exposed group compared with a holdout; at reports and &quot;not interested&quot; actions; at notification opt-outs; and at survey measures of whether people feel their time was well spent. If retention is flat or lower and negative feedback is up, the time is a short-term effect. Fourth, the second-order effects. Rankings that raise time spent often favor sensational content, and over time users who feel worse leave or reduce use. Advertisers may buy fewer impressions of poor quality. So: good if the extra time is in meaningful interaction, retention and satisfaction hold, and negative feedback does not rise; a warning if it comes from passive, high-arousal content. As a documented example of a company choosing to move this metric down on purpose, CS-C05-02 describes Facebook's 2018 decision. I'd propose making &quot;time spent in interactions that users later say they valued&quot; the primary measure for this change, with time spent as an input, and I'd run the test with a long-term holdout. I'd change my view if the holdout shows higher 90-day retention for the exposed group.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Choose a north star metric for a two-sided marketplace that connects freelancers with clients, and defend it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Gross revenue&quot; or &quot;number of users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names candidates, tests them against criteria, chooses one with a definition, and adds inputs and guardrails for both sides.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Addresses quality and liquidity, shows why the obvious candidates fail, defines the metric precisely, handles the two sides separately, and says what would change the choice as the marketplace matures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The value of this marketplace is a good match: a client gets work done well and a freelancer gets paid for it. Candidates: 1. Gross merchandise value (total paid): fails the value test because it rises with higher prices or longer projects, and fails the gaming test. 2. Number of registered users: a vanity metric that rises by construction. 3. Contracts started per week: better, but a contract that ends badly counts as a success. 4. Successful contracts per week: contracts that were completed and rated satisfactory by both sides, counted in the week they completed. I choose the fourth. Definition: a contract is successful if the work was delivered, the client released payment without a dispute, and the client rated it at least four of five (an assumed threshold). The numerator is successful contracts completed in the week, and the unit is the contract. It passes the tests: it reflects value for both sides, the team can move it through matching and trust features, it moves in weeks, and it is hard to inflate without genuine completion. Inputs, by side: demand (clients posting jobs, jobs receiving qualified proposals, time to first proposal), supply (freelancers active, proposals per job), and match quality (share of jobs filled, hire rate). Guardrails: dispute rate, freelancer earnings per hour, client repeat rate, and the share of payments made through the platform (to detect leakage). Counter metric: the rate of jobs filled by a freelancer with very few ratings, which a push for fill rate might raise at the expense of quality. I'd revisit as the marketplace matures: when most jobs are filled, the constraint shifts from liquidity to quality, and a measure of repeat contracts between the same pair would carry more weight. I'd also watch for a gap between sides: a metric that rises because clients are well served while freelancers earn less would be a warning.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c05-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C05-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> metrics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C05 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A support team's quarterly goal is &quot;tickets closed.&quot; You suspect the number is being gamed. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Tell the team to stop gaming and be honest.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Looks for evidence of gaming, proposes counter metrics, and redefines the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats gaming as a system response not a character flaw, proposes a metric set that makes the cheap path visible, handles the change with the team, and states what outcome the team should actually be measured on.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start from the assumption that people are responding sensibly to what they are measured on, so the question is what the metric rewards. First, test the suspicion with data, not accusation. Look for signs: closure times that cluster just before a deadline; tickets closed and reopened; a rise in the share of tickets closed as &quot;resolved&quot; with no reply; a growing number of repeat contacts about the same issue; closure counts rising while customer satisfaction is flat or falling. If I see repeat contact up and satisfaction down, gaming is likely. Second, find what &quot;tickets closed&quot; rewards. It rewards closing easy tickets first and closing hard ones quickly, whether or not they were resolved. A <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a> is that hard tickets age and customers contact again. Third, redesign the metric set. The goal should be customers whose problem is solved. A primary metric could be the resolution rate: tickets resolved without a repeat contact within seven days, divided by tickets closed. Counters: customer satisfaction on resolved tickets, and the age of the oldest open tickets (to stop easy-first cherry-picking). Keep closed tickets as an input (volume), not the target. Fourth, handle it with the team. I'd show them the data about repeat contacts, explain that the old target made the bad behavior rational, and agree the new set with their input. I'd also audit a sample of closures by hand for the first two months, and I'd avoid punishing the old behavior, since the old target created it. I'd change my approach if the review shows no gaming: then the question is whether closed tickets is the right measure anyway, and the answer is probably still to add a quality counter.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Customers keep asking us to add a &quot;bulk edit&quot; feature to our project-management tool. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it, since customers are asking,&quot; or &quot;ask customers what they want.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Steps up from the request to the job, checks who is asking, weighs the evidence, and offers more than one solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates segments among the requesters, uses behavior as well as requests, proposes the cheapest test, and says what would make the answer no.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat &quot;bulk edit&quot; as a solution and look for the problem under it. I'd ask the people who requested it to walk me through the last time they needed to change many items at once: what were they doing, how many items, how often, what did they do instead? Common workarounds are exporting to a spreadsheet and re-importing, or editing items one by one. Then I'd check who is asking. If the requests come mostly from team leads managing hundreds of tasks, it is a different segment from a casual user with a dozen tasks. I'd look at behavior: how many users perform repeated single edits in a row (a signal that bulk edit would help), and what share of tickets or sales notes mention it. Suppose the interviews show the real job is &quot;after a planning meeting, I want to move twenty tasks to a new sprint and reassign owners, so I can leave with everything in order.&quot; Bulk edit is one solution. Others are a &quot;move a group&quot; action, a planning view that allows drag and drop, or a template for sprint planning. They differ in cost, and the narrower ones may serve the job. My recommendation is to ship the narrowest version that serves the job for the team-lead segment, and test it with five customers who asked, with the criterion that at least four use it in their next planning meeting. The cost is that other bulk operations wait. I'd change my mind if the behavior data show that few users perform repeated edits, in which case the requests are loud but not widespread.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A language-learning app serves students, working professionals and retirees. Which segment would you build for first?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Students, since there are the most of them,&quot; with no criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by need, scores on stated criteria, chooses one, and names what it gives up.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notes the segments may be defined by a job (not an age), tests the choice with evidence, and says what would change it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll first note that age is a weak guide to need. What matters is why each person is learning, so I'd restate the segments by job: students learning for exams, professionals learning for a move or a role abroad, and retirees learning for travel and enjoyment. I'll score them on size, pain intensity, reach and our right to win (FW-C06-02), with rough assumed values. | Segment (by job) | Size | Pain intensity | Reach | Fit and right to win | |---|---|---|---|---| | Exam learners | High | Medium (deadline, but school provides structure) | Medium (crowded channels) | Medium | | Professionals relocating | Medium | High (a date, real stakes, willingness to pay) | High (can reach through employers and expat forums) | High (needs practical speaking, which the product can deliver) | | Retirees learning for travel | Medium | Low to medium (no deadline) | Low (different channels) | Medium | I choose professionals who are relocating. The deciding criterion is pain intensity combined with reach: they have a deadline and they pay, and we can reach them directly. I give up the size of the exam-learner segment and the long-term loyalty of retirees. Evidence I'd collect to confirm: interviews with eight people who relocated in the past year about how they prepared, which tools they used and what failed; the share who paid for tutoring; and a landing-page test for a &quot;speak at work in 30 days&quot; offer. I'd change my choice if interviews showed that relocating professionals mostly rely on employer-provided classes, which would shrink our opening.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> People don't use our calendar app's scheduling assistant. How would you find out why?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Survey users to ask why they don't use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments non-users, lists hypotheses, combines behavioral data, interviews and a survey, and defines the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates &quot;never saw it,&quot; &quot;tried and left&quot; and &quot;doesn't need it,&quot; sets a decision rule, and addresses sampling bias against people who have already left.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> First the decision: do we invest in the assistant, change how it is discovered, or retire it? I'd also define &quot;don't use&quot;: say 4% of weekly users have used it in the last month (assumed), against an expectation of 15%. The non-users are three different groups, and the research should separate them: people who never saw the assistant (discovery), people who tried it and stopped (a usability or value problem), and people who looked and decided they don't need it (a need problem). Each leads to a different action. Behavioral data comes first and is cheap: what share of weekly users saw the entry point, what share of those started it, what share of starters completed a scheduled meeting, and what share used it again. Say exposure is 20%, start rate 30% of those, completion 50% of starters. Then a user who sees it has a 15% chance of finishing once (0.30 × 0.50), and only 20% see it, so overall 3% finish (0.20 × 0.15), close to the observed 4%. The biggest loss is exposure. Interviews come second: eight people from each group, asking them to describe the last time they scheduled a meeting with several people, how they did it, and what they tried. For the &quot;tried and left&quot; group, I'd ask them to show me what happened. A short survey third, built from the interviews, would size how common each reason is. Sampling is the danger: people who left the app are not in my active-user list, so I'd recruit some lapsed users separately. My decision rule: if the main loss is exposure and those who try it complete meetings, fix discovery; if many who try it abandon, fix the experience; if most say they don't schedule multi-person meetings, retire it. I'd change the plan if the need turns out to be concentrated in a segment such as team admins, and I'd focus on them.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a survey, 35% of customers say they would pay $15 a month for a new feature. Do you believe it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 35% is a strong signal,&quot; or &quot;No, surveys are unreliable.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the say-do gap, computes the margin of error, asks about sampling, and proposes a behavioral test.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Estimates a discount factor, tests with a commitment, converts the result into a revenue range, and says what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat the 35% as the upper bound of interest, not a forecast, for three reasons. First, it is a stated intention about future behavior, which is where the say-do gap is largest. People say yes to hypothetical purchases because saying yes costs nothing. Second, sampling: how many responded and who? Suppose 300 responded out of 2,000 invited (assumed), a 15% response rate, so the respondents are more interested than the typical customer. The margin of error on 35% from 300 responses is about 1.96 × the square root of (0.35 × 0.65 ÷ 300) = about 5.4 points, so the survey says 30% to 40% of respondents, before any non-response bias. Third, price and framing: how was the feature described, and was the price shown at the moment of choice? To get a behavioral number, I'd run a commitment test: show the offer with a real checkout to a sample, or take refundable pre-orders. Suppose 5,000 users see the offer (assumed) and 1.5% pay. Then the working conversion is about 1.5%, and the stated-to-actual ratio is about 23 (35% ÷ 1.5%). I'd use that ratio, labeled as specific to this audience and offer, to discount future surveys. For 50,000 active customers (assumed), 1.5% is 750 payers, at $15 a month that is $135,000 a year. The survey implied 17,500 payers, or $3.15 million a year, so the forecast error would have been more than twenty-fold. I'd decide on the behavioral number, and I'd say what would change the plan: a price test or a different packaging that raises conversion above about 4%, which is the level at which the feature covers a small team (assumed).</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have two weeks and no research budget. How would you decide whether to build a group-ordering feature in a food-delivery app?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Survey customers and see if they want it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists assumptions, picks the riskiest, designs two or three cheap tests, and writes criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Combines existing data, interviews and a commitment test, writes a decision rule in advance, and names what the plan cannot tell.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd list what must be true: that groups regularly coordinate orders and find it hard (desirability); that a shared cart is a better fix than what they do today; that groups order often enough to matter (viability); and that it is buildable (feasibility). The riskiest is the first, because if groups do not struggle, the rest does not matter. Days 1 to 3: use existing data. Orders with three or more items to one address at one time give a rough proxy for group orders: say 4% of orders (assumed). Read about 100 support tickets for coordination complaints. Days 3 to 8: eight interviews with customers who placed large orders recently, asking about the last time they ordered for a group, how they coordinated, what went wrong, and what they did about it. Days 6 to 12: a fake door. Show a &quot;Start a group order&quot; button to 3% of customers who recently ordered three or more items, leading to a &quot;coming soon, tell us what you need&quot; page with a short form. Days 10 to 14: a concierge test. Coordinate ten group orders by hand through messages. Before running anything, I'd write the criteria. At least five of eight interviewees describe a recent coordination struggle. At least 4% of the exposed click the fake door. At least seven of ten concierge orders complete with three or more participants. Rule: all three, build a thin version; two, iterate and retest; one or none, stop. The plan cannot tell us how group ordering performs at scale, or whether a built version would match a hand-run one. It tests the assumption most likely to be wrong, cheaply. I'd change the plan if the proxy shows group orders are well under 1% of orders: the opportunity would be too small to justify the time.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your interviews say users want a simpler interface, your survey says they want more features, and your analytics show most users use only three. How do you reconcile them?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pick the one I trust most&quot; or &quot;average them.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks who each source covers, what each can and cannot show, and identifies the segments.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that the sources may describe different segments, generates a testable hypothesis, and proposes a decision that serves each segment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I wouldn't pick one source. I'd ask what each can and cannot show, and who it covers. The interviews show reasons, from a few people. Who did I recruit? If I interviewed beginners, &quot;simpler&quot; is natural. The survey shows what many say they want, which is stated, not behavior, and people often ask for more features while using few. The analytics show behavior across everyone: most use three features. That is consistent with a simpler interface being enough for most. The apparent conflict may be a segment difference. Perhaps most users are casual and need the three features to be easy, while a minority of heavy users want many features. I'd test it by splitting each source by usage level: do heavy users account for the &quot;more features&quot; answers in the survey, and are the interviewees casual users? Suppose the split shows that 80% of users use three features and most of the &quot;more features&quot; answers come from the heavy 10% (assumed). Then both sources are right about different groups. The hypothesis is that a simple default with an advanced mode serves both. My decision would be to simplify the default experience for the majority, since the behavior says that is where the volume is, and to offer progressive access to more features for heavy users, tested with a small group of each. The cost is added complexity of two modes. I would change my view if heavy users turn out to drive a large share of revenue or retention, in which case their needs weigh more than their numbers suggest.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c06-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C06-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C06 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time user research changed your mind.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A story where the research confirmed what the candidate already thought.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the original belief, the method, what the evidence showed, and the decision that changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows how the candidate designed the research to be able to disprove the belief, how quickly they updated, the cost of the change, and what they now do differently.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Illustrative: a composite story; numbers invented.</em> &quot;I was the PM for a recipe app, and I believed that users would want a meal-planning calendar. It was my idea, and several colleagues liked it. Before building, I wrote down what would change my mind: if fewer than half of the people I interviewed planned meals at all, I would drop it. &quot;I interviewed ten regular users, asking about the last week of dinners. Only three planned meals in advance. The other seven decided that afternoon, usually while commuting, and the common frustration was not planning but 'what can I make with what's in my kitchen.' I also checked behavior: 61% of recipe views happened between 4pm and 6pm (invented). &quot;I dropped the calendar. I told the team the same day, with the numbers, and proposed a 'cook tonight' view that starts from the ingredients people have. It took us a week to prototype. In a test with 200 users, 38% of those who saw it cooked a recipe from it that evening against 22% for the standard home screen (invented). &quot;The cost was a month of discussion I had spent socializing the calendar idea, and the disappointment of a colleague who had designed screens for it. What I do differently now is write the disconfirming criterion before the first interview, and I show the team the research plan before the results, so that a change of direction does not feel like a loss for anyone.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c07-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C07-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C07 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a feature for a smartwatch aimed at people training for their first marathon.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists features (heart-rate zones, a training plan, music) with no user choice, no problem and no decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Chooses a specific user, ranks pain points, offers solutions of different kinds, recommends one with a stated cost, and gives a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the watch's strengths (sensing, haptics, glanceability), addresses injury and wellbeing risk, keeps data on the device, and says what would change the recommendation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume this is a feature in the watch's existing fitness software, paired with a phone, and that the goal is regular, safe use of the watch during training. Goal ladder: the company wants the watch worn and used every day; the product goal is more first-time marathon trainers who complete their plan; the runner's goal is to finish a first marathon without getting hurt. One tension: pushing runners to train harder may raise use and raise injury risk, so I'll weigh safety above engagement. <em>User.</em> Four segments: first-time marathon trainers, experienced racers, casual runners and run-walkers. I choose first-time trainers. Their pain is most acute (a long plan with no experience to fall back on), they have the least support, and a watch is the tool they carry on every run. I give up experienced racers, who are a larger and better-served group. Persona: Maya follows a 16-week plan, runs before work, and checks her wrist mid-run (invented). <em>Pain points.</em> | Pain point | Frequency | Severity | Workaround today | Rank | |---|---|---|---|---| | Going out too fast in the first third of a long run and fading | Every long run, weekly | High (a bad long run costs confidence) | Checks pace constantly; runs with a friend | 1 | | Not knowing whether to push through soreness or rest | Weekly in the heavy weeks | High (injury) | Searches online; asks friends | 2 | | A fixed plan that breaks when life interrupts | Several times in 16 weeks | Medium | Skips and loses the thread | 3 | | Fueling and hydration timing on long runs | Every long run | Medium | Guesses | 4 | | Motivation sagging in the middle weeks | Weeks 6 to 12 | Medium | Quits or repeats the plan | 5 | I choose the first two as one theme, effort control, because both come from a beginner who cannot yet feel her own limits. I'd confirm the ranking by asking ten recent first-time finishers about their worst long run and their worst week, and I'd expect pacing to rank above fueling. <em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Eliminate: the plan sets the pace for each mile, so the runner never decides. Automate: the watch adjusts the target from heart rate and fatigue signals in real time. Assist: a tap on the wrist when pace is 10% faster than plan, with a different pattern for too slow. Reframe: train by effort level (&quot;conversational pace&quot;) and not by pace, since pace is a poor guide for a beginner on hills and in heat. Connect: a friend or coach pacing remotely. <em>Evaluation.</em> Eliminate is simple and rigid. Automate is the most powerful, needs reliable signals and can be wrong, and a wrong target costs a beginner a run. Assist is cheap and uses the watch's strength, which is the wrist. Reframe addresses the root of beginners' trouble. Connect depends on other people. I recommend Assist with the Reframe: haptic pacing by effort, for long runs. It gives the runner a calm, glanceless signal at the moment she is drifting, by giving up adaptive plans in the first version. Next I'd add a mid-run effort check, and then plan adjustment after a missed week. <em>First version as moments.</em> Before the run, the plan sets a target effort range for the day. Mile three: if her effort is above the range for two minutes, the watch gives a slow double tap. If she settles, a single quiet tap confirms it. Mile twelve: if she drifts below, a different pattern. After the run, a one-line summary: &quot;You held your range for 82% of the run&quot; (invented). <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of planned long runs completed within the target effort range for at least 70% of the run (invented threshold). Input: the share of haptic alerts followed by a return to range within two minutes. Guardrails: the share of runners who switch alerts off within two weeks, and battery use over a long run. Counter metric: the share of runs cut short, since a runner might stop to satisfy the range and not the body. <em>Risks.</em> Safety: the watch is not a medical device and must never tell a runner to push through pain; any effort advice is capped, and a high-effort signal suggests easing off. Wellbeing: no shame in the summary. Privacy: heart-rate and effort data are processed on the watch and kept on the user's devices by default, with sharing only on opt-in. I'd change my mind if first-time finishers said their problem was fueling and not pacing, in which case the first version becomes a timing assistant. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that design prompts at Apple often involve its own devices and weigh privacy and fit with the ecosystem <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Apple data or product behavior is described or implied.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c07-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C07-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C07 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design an alarm clock for people who are deaf.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Use flashing lights and vibration,&quot; with no user detail, no problem and no recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Notes that the user is given, spends time on the problem and its failure cost, offers different kinds of solutions, and recommends one.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats reliability as the central requirement, designs a backup, considers the sleeping partner and travel, and reframes the design as one that benefits everyone.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The user is given, so I'll skip segmentation, confirm the user, and spend the time on the problem. &quot;Deaf&quot; covers a range: people who are deaf, hard of hearing and use hearing aids (which they usually remove at night), and people who use sign language as a first language. The common fact is that a sound alarm will not wake them. I'd confirm that with a quick check of how they wake up today. <em>Goal.</em> The company wants a physical product that people trust and recommend; the user's goal is to wake on time, every time, without help from another person. <em>Pain points.</em> | Pain point | Frequency | Severity | Workaround today | Rank | |---|---|---|---|---| | A cue strong enough to wake from deep sleep | Every night | Very high (a missed morning costs work or a flight) | Asks a partner; sleeps lightly; uses several alarms | 1 | | Trusting the alarm: a dead battery or a failed alarm is silent | Every night, as a worry | High | Checks the alarm repeatedly | 2 | | Waking a partner who can hear | Every morning if there is one | Medium | Sets the cue on a wristband | 3 | | Travel: hotel rooms offer no equivalent | Per trip | High | Brings a device; asks the front desk | 4 | | The body adjusts to one cue and stops responding | Over weeks | Medium | Changes the alarm | 5 | I choose the first two. The central requirement of an alarm is reliability, so the design must make failure visible before sleep. <em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Assist: a wristband that vibrates at the set time. Automate: a bed shaker, which is stronger, that starts weakly and builds. Reframe: a lamp that brightens over 20 minutes and works with the body's waking cycle, so that the alarm is a gradual cue. Connect: a call or message to a friend or relative as a last resort. Eliminate: a combination that removes the worry itself, by checking its own state every night. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend a wristband as the primary cue, with a bed shaker as backup and the phone as a last resort, in the first version. This gives up elegance (three parts) to buy reliability. Before sleep, the wristband shows a green light for &quot;armed and charged,&quot; and a red light if anything is wrong. Next I'd add the lamp for a gentler wake, and travel pairing so the wristband works from the phone in a hotel. I'd drop the backup only if testing showed the wristband alone woke people more than 99.9% of the time (invented), and I would not drop the green-light check. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of alarms acknowledged by touch within three minutes, as a proxy for waking on time. Input: the share of nights the device shows &quot;armed&quot; before the user sleeps. Guardrail: the failure-to-fire rate, which must be near zero and is the metric that matters most; battery-related failures. Counter metric: partner-disturbance reports, so the design does not fix one problem and create another. <em>Risks.</em> Reliability is the safety risk, and the design answer is the self-check and the redundancy. Skin comfort for a wristband worn all night. Dignity: the product should not be marketed or shaped as a device &quot;for deaf people&quot;; heavy sleepers, shift workers and people sharing a bed with a light sleeper would all want a silent, strong cue, which is the curb-cut effect (section 9). I'd change my mind if users said they already trusted their phone's vibration and wanted software, not hardware.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c07-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C07-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C07 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a feature in a maps app for people who use wheelchairs.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Show wheelchair-accessible routes,&quot; with no analysis of data, failure or segments.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies what makes a route accessible, ranks the pain points, addresses where the data comes from, and recommends a version.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees the failure cost (being stranded), shows confidence rather than false certainty, uses community and partner data with safeguards, and avoids inferring disability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume a feature in an existing maps app on phones, with the goal that people trust the app for everyday trips. The user is given as people who use wheelchairs, but it spans manual and powered chairs, and people with other step-free needs (strollers, walking limits, heavy luggage) share part of the need. I'll design for wheelchair users first, as the extreme user whose needs are strict: one step or one steep slope blocks the route. <em>Pain points.</em> | Pain point | Frequency | Severity | Workaround today | Rank | |---|---|---|---|---| | A route that looks fine on the map has a step, a steep ramp or construction | Weekly for active users (invented) | Very high (stranded far from home) | Scouts in advance; avoids new places | 1 | | Missing information: many places have no step-free data | Most new trips | High | Calls the venue | 2 | | The accessible entrance is not the main entrance | Common at older buildings | Medium | Searches for the side entrance | 3 | | Lift outages on transit | Occasional | High | Checks the operator's site | 4 | | Routes that are technically step-free but much longer | Often | Medium | Accepts it | 5 | I choose the first two. They are one problem: the app cannot yet tell the user how much to trust a route. Wrong information is worse than none, because a confident wrong route strands a person. <em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Automate: infer step-free paths from street imagery and sensor data. Assist: step-free routing that labels each route with how sure the app is and where the information came from. Reframe: plan to the accessible entrance, not the street address. Connect: let users and venues confirm and correct data. Eliminate: not possible for the app alone; I'd recommend that venues and cities publish the data. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend Assist with Connect for the first version: step-free routing with confidence labels, in a few areas where data is good, with a one-tap way to report a blocker. It gives users an honest route by giving up broad coverage at launch. Next I'd add the accessible-entrance guidance and lift status; then automated inference to widen coverage, with human confirmation for what it adds. I'd bring the inference forward if reports showed the data gaps were the dominant failure. <em>First version as moments.</em> The user turns on &quot;step-free&quot; in settings once. Each route shows &quot;Confirmed step-free&quot; (a recent user or venue confirmation), &quot;Likely step-free&quot; (inferred), or &quot;Unknown on this segment,&quot; which the user can inspect on a map. During the trip, one tap reports &quot;blocked here.&quot; The report corrects the data and warns the next user within minutes. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of step-free routes completed without a reported blocker. Input: the share of route length with confirmed data in the launch area. Guardrails: the blocker-report rate per 1,000 routes, and the time from a report to a corrected route. Counter metric: abandonment of routes labeled &quot;unknown,&quot; which tells us whether honest uncertainty drives people away or whether they accept it. <em>Risks.</em> False assurance: a &quot;confirmed&quot; label must expire and be re-checked, and the app must not show certainty it lacks. Data abuse: crowd contributions can be wrong or malicious, so changes need corroboration. Privacy: the app must not infer that someone has a disability from how they move, and the setting is a choice, not a guess; contribution is opt-in, and location trails are not stored for it. I'd change my mind if wheelchair users said that they wanted the app mainly for the venues' entrance information, in which case the first version is a venue layer and not routing. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that product-design prompts at Google can concern its own products such as maps, often at global scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google product behavior is described or implied.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c07-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C07-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C07 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a feature to help people who join a meeting remotely feel included when others are together in a room.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Better cameras and a chat window,&quot; with no analysis of the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the remote participant's specific problems, ranks them, offers different kinds of solutions and measures inclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Chooses a metric that captures equity without forcing participation, protects people in the room, and considers the organizer's role.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume a feature in an existing video-meeting product, used at work, on a laptop for remote people and a shared screen and camera in the room. The goal: remote participation that is as effective as in-room. Goal ladder: the company wants the product to be the default for hybrid work; the product goal is balanced participation; the remote person's goal is to be heard and to follow what is happening. <em>User.</em> Four roles: the remote participant, the in-room participant, the meeting organizer and the person who sets up the room. I choose the remote participant, because their disadvantage is structural and they cannot fix it themselves. I'll note the organizer as the person who can change the meeting's behavior, since a feature that depends on the organizer's discipline will fail. <em>Pain points.</em> | Pain point | Frequency | Severity | Workaround today | Rank | |---|---|---|---|---| | Cannot jump into the conversation: the room speaks over the gaps | Most hybrid meetings | High | Waits; types in chat; stays quiet | 1 | | Cannot hear side conversations or the quiet speaker in the room | Often | High | Asks &quot;can you repeat that?&quot; | 2 | | Cannot see the whiteboard or who is speaking | Often | Medium | Asks someone to share a photo | 3 | | Chat is missed by the room | Often | Medium | Hopes a person in the room reads it | 4 | | Feels invisible after the meeting: decisions made in the corridor | Occasional | Medium | Follows up in messages | 5 | I choose the first two. They are the heart of exclusion: the remote person cannot enter and cannot hear. <em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Eliminate: a &quot;remote-first&quot; mode where everyone joins on their own device, with room speakers muted, so nobody has an advantage. Automate: a camera that frames and follows the active speaker, with audio that favors the quietest voices. Assist: a visible queue so remote people can raise a hand and the room sees a light or an on-screen line. Reframe: a rotating &quot;remote advocate&quot; role in the room. Connect: a post-meeting summary of decisions for everyone. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend the queue with speaker framing for the first version. It gives remote people a way into the conversation without depending on anyone's goodwill by giving up the full fairness of remote-first, which needs a changed habit. Next I'd add the post-meeting decision summary, and offer remote-first as a mode organizers can choose. I'd bring remote-first forward if organizers adopted it readily. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the ratio of remote participants' share of speaking turns to their share of attendees, with a target near one. Input: the share of raised-hand requests acknowledged within 30 seconds. Guardrails: meeting length, and in-room participants' satisfaction, so that the feature does not slow meetings down or annoy the room. Counter metric: forced turns, such as a remote person being called on when they did not raise a hand, since the aim is the option to speak, not a quota. <em>Risks.</em> Privacy: room cameras and microphones capture people who did not join the call, so the room device shows a clear recording indicator and a consent step. Wellbeing: a speaking-time metric must not be turned into an individual score for performance reviews, so it is shown in aggregate to the organizer only. Accessibility: captions on by default for the remote side, and the queue works by keyboard and voice. I'd change my mind if the data showed that remote people's participation was already balanced and the problem was information, not speaking.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c07-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C07-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C07 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a way for family members to share location with strong privacy controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A live map of the family with a privacy toggle,&quot; with no analysis of misuse or consent.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the real need, builds consent into the design, covers vulnerable users (children and people in controlling relationships) and sets protective defaults.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shares the minimum that serves the need (events and time limits before a constant map), protects the person who pauses, sets a launch condition or kill criterion, and recognizes that a feature can be a tool of control.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll design this from first principles, not describe any existing product. I'll assume a feature on phones and watches that a family can turn on, with a goal of helping families coordinate and feel safe. Goal ladder: the company wants a family to find the platform useful and trustworthy; the product goal is that the feature makes family life easier without a loss of trust; each person's goal differs, and that is the central difficulty. A parent wants to know a child got home. A teenager wants privacy. An older parent wants help without losing independence. In an abusive relationship, location sharing can be a tool of control. I'll put the responsible-design check (FW-C07-06) first, because it shapes the design, not just the end of the answer. <em>User.</em> By situation: parents of younger children (about 8 to 12), parents of teenagers, adults checking on an aging parent, and partners. I choose parents of children about 8 to 12 as the first group. The need is clear (arrival, pickups), the child has little reason to hide, and the family structure makes consent simpler. I give up partners and teenagers for the first version, since the misuse and consent issues are hardest there, and I'd treat them as separate designs and not as a toggle on the first. <em>Pain points</em> (family coordination; I'd verify with interviews). | Pain point | Frequency | Severity | Workaround today | Rank | |---|---|---|---|---| | &quot;Are you there yet?&quot; messages around travel | Daily | Low to medium | Texts and calls | 1 | | Worry between a child leaving and arriving | Daily | Medium to high | Waits for a call | 2 | | Pickup coordination | Several times a week | Medium | Calls and waits | 3 | | A person cannot tell who can see their location | If sharing exists, always | High (trust) | Turns it off | 4 | | Sharing used to control another person | Rare, severe | Very high | None for the person affected | 5 | The first three show what the need really is: arrival, not constant tracking. I choose to design around events and trips, and treat the last two as design requirements, not ranked problems, because their severity is too high to trade against convenience. <em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Eliminate: no location sharing; an &quot;arrived&quot; message only. Automate: automatic sharing along a regular route. Assist: sharing for a set trip or time, which ends by itself. Reframe: share the arrival time (an ETA) and not the position. Connect: a shared place list (&quot;home,&quot; &quot;school&quot;) with arrival and departure notices. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend the combination of Reframe and Assist in the first version: arrival and departure notices for places the family agrees on, plus time-limited sharing for a single trip, with no always-on map by default. It gives families the thing they asked for with the least exposure, by giving up the simplicity of a constant live view. Next I'd add an optional continuous view for families who choose it, with the controls below; and later, versions for teenagers and for adults and aging parents, each with its own consent flow. <em>The design, by the checks.</em> <em>Consent:</em> nothing is shared until each person accepts, and each person sees the same thing the others see about them. <em>Visibility:</em> a persistent indicator shows when sharing is active and with whom. <em>Control:</em> a one-tap pause or stop for the person being shared, and sharing for trips ends automatically. <em>Protection for the person who pauses:</em> others see &quot;location unavailable,&quot; which looks the same whether sharing is paused, the phone is off or there is no signal, so that pausing does not expose someone in an unsafe situation, and the design discusses openly the cost, which is that a parent may worry. <em>Children:</em> age-based defaults, with a child's consent step written in plain words, and the parent's controls stop at the minimum needed. <em>Data:</em> the minimum location history, stored on the user's devices where possible, with a short retention period, and not used for advertising. <em>Misuse:</em> an easy way to exit a sharing relationship and to find out who has been sharing with you, and a route to support for someone being tracked without consent. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of sharing relationships still active after 90 days in which both people report that the arrangement is comfortable, from a short survey. Input: the share of relationships set up with places and trips rather than continuous sharing. Guardrails: abuse and harassment reports per 10,000 relationships, and the time to resolve each. Counter metric: the share of relationships ended in the first week, which could mean friction is too high, or could mean the feature surfaced a conflict that needed a conversation. <em>Launch condition.</em> I would not launch if any path allowed sharing to begin without the person's knowledge, or if testing with safety experts found a way to use the feature covertly that we could not close. I'd also hold the launch if the abuse-report rate in a limited release exceeded a threshold set in advance. I'd change my recommendation if research showed that families wanted a constant view badly enough that they set it up anyway on a worse product; then the better course is a well-designed continuous option with strong defaults. Rules about children's data and consent differ by country <code>[V: rules differ by country, for example India's DPDP Act treats anyone under 18 as a child]</code>, so I'd involve legal and safety teams from the start. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple publishes a privacy position and <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that its design prompts weigh privacy and safety <code>[V: Apple privacy pages; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. This answer does not describe how any existing product works.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c07-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C07-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C07 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a smart pill bottle for older adults who take many medications.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A bottle that reminds you to take your pills and tells your family if you miss one.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the real problems, considers non-device alternatives, offers different kinds of solutions and sees the safety stakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notes what the device can and cannot know (opening is not swallowing), plans for failure and battery, treats hardware as hard to change, and tests before building.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume a hardware-and-app product, sold to older adults or to their adult children, with a goal of taking medications as prescribed. The company's goal is a product that people and doctors trust; the user's goal is to stay well and independent. Because it is hardware that touches health, I'll put reliability and safety in the first minutes. <em>User.</em> Segments by situation: older adults managing five or more daily medications on their own, those whose adult child helps, and those with a paid caregiver. I choose people managing on their own with a family member who worries, because the pain is daily, the stakes are health, and the family is the buyer. I give up the caregiver market, which has different buyers and rules. There are two users: the person who takes the pills, and the person who worries. <em>Pain points.</em> | Pain point | Frequency | Severity | Workaround today | Rank | |---|---|---|---|---| | Forgetting a dose, or doubting whether one was taken | Several times a week | High | Pillbox with days; a note | 1 | | Taking a dose twice | Occasional | Very high | Checks the pillbox | 2 | | Confusing similar pills or several bottles | Daily | High | Labels, memory | 3 | | Running out of a medication | Monthly | High | Calendar reminders | 4 | | A family member who cannot tell how things are going | Weekly | Medium | Calls | 5 | I choose the first two, which share a cause: no reliable record of what was taken, and no gentle cue. <em>Solutions that differ in kind.</em> Eliminate: the pharmacy prepares packs sorted by day and time, so there is nothing to sort and nothing to confuse; this needs no electronics. Automate: a dispenser that releases the right pills at the right time. Assist: a smart cap with a light and chime that records when the bottle is opened. Reframe: tie doses to a daily routine, such as meals or brushing teeth, with a cue placed where the routine happens. Connect: an alert to a family member when a dose looks missed. <em>Evaluation and recommendation.</em> The strongest first move is often the cheapest one, and I'd say so: the pharmacy packs solve confusion without any new hardware. For the device, the assist-style cap is cheaper and simpler than a dispenser, which is expensive, bulky and risky if it releases the wrong pills. I recommend a combined first version: the cap with light and chime, working with pharmacy packs where the pharmacy supports them, and a gentle, optional family summary. It gives a cue and a record by giving up the dispenser's automation. Next I'd add refill reminders, then the dispenser for people with the most complex regimens. <em>What the device can and cannot know.</em> The cap knows it was opened, not that the pill was swallowed. A &quot;dose taken&quot; label would be a claim the device cannot support, so it says &quot;bottle opened&quot; and the app asks, &quot;Did you take it?&quot; before it tells anyone anything. An opening is a proxy for a dose (a <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a> in C05's terms), and the design must not treat it as the dose. <em>Failure and hardware.</em> If the battery dies, the bottle must still work as a normal bottle, and a low battery alert goes out days earlier. Because hardware cannot easily be changed after shipping, I'd fix the safety behaviors in software that can be updated, and keep the physical design simple. I'd run a concierge prototype before manufacturing: 20 households (invented) with pre-sorted packs and daily text prompts for four weeks, to learn which cue the person responds to and whether the family summary helps or irritates. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of scheduled doses with a bottle opening or a confirmed &quot;taken&quot; within a window set by the prescriber's instructions. Input: the share of users who respond to the cue within 15 minutes. Guardrails: failure-to-alert events (missed alarms from battery or connection faults, which should be close to zero) and double-open events. Counter metric: the rate at which people stop using the cap after the first month, since a reminder that nags gets abandoned. <em>Risks.</em> Safety: wrong or late alerts could cause harm, so the app never advises on dosing and always sends questions about dosage to the person's clinician or pharmacist. Dignity and consent: the family summary is the older person's choice and shown to them. Privacy: medication records are sensitive health data, so they are stored with the minimum needed and shared only by opt-in. Regulation: a device that makes health claims may fall under medical-device rules that vary by country <code>[V: FDA, EU MDR and India's Medical Devices Rules 2017 each regulate software with a medical purpose]</code>, so the claims stay modest. I'd change my mind if the prototype showed that pharmacy packs alone solved the problem, in which case the product is the pack, and the cap is unnecessary.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c07-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C07-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C07 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a product that helps people reduce food waste at home. <em>(Midway through your answer, the interviewer says: &quot;I'm not sure your user is the biggest source of waste. Why not restaurants?&quot;)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Gets defensive or abandons the choice at once, and has no criteria to settle the question.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats the challenge as a chance to restate the criteria, defends or revises the choice with reasons, and keeps the spine.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the size of the problem from the product's right to win, states what evidence would change the choice, answers the challenge in under a minute, and addresses the safety risk of the design.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>The first part of the answer, in brief.</em> I'll assume a consumer app from a company with a grocery or a smart-home reach, and a goal of helping people waste less food and save money. I choose households that cook most weeknight meals and shop weekly, because the waste is regular and they can change it. Pain points, ranked: buying more than they use, forgetting what is in the fridge until it spoils, not knowing whether food is still safe, and cooking portions that are too large. I choose the first two as one problem, buying and storing without a view of what is already there. <em>The challenge.</em> &quot;That's a fair challenge, and the way to answer it is by the criteria I used for the user: size, pain, reach and fit. On size, UNEP's Food Waste Index Report 2024 puts households as the largest source of food waste in 2022 (60 percent), ahead of food services (28 percent) and retail (12 percent), though the split depends on the country <code>[V: UNEP and WRAP, Food Waste Index Report 2024: 2022 data, households 60 percent, food services 28 percent, retail 12 percent; 1.05 billion tonnes]</code>. If it were the other way, that alone would not decide it, because restaurants and households differ in the other criteria. A restaurant has a strong money reason to cut waste and a small number of decision-makers, so a B2B tool can reach it directly. A household has a weak reason for any single meal, and many decision-makers, which is why a consumer product is harder but also less served. Fit decides the rest. I assumed a consumer company with reach into homes, which favors households. If the company were a restaurant-supply business, I'd switch. So my position is that I'd keep households for this prompt, and the evidence that would move me is the company's strengths and a size figure in this country that put restaurants far ahead.&quot; <em>Then the answer continues.</em> Solutions that differ in kind: Eliminate: a shopping list built from what is already in the kitchen, so less is bought. Automate: the fridge or the receipt identifies what is there. Assist: an alert two days before an item's date with a recipe that uses it. Reframe: plan meals around what must be used, &quot;use-first&quot; cooking. Connect: share or swap surplus with neighbors. I recommend the Assist and Eliminate pair for the first version, a use-first list with one nudge per day, because it needs no new hardware and acts at the moment a decision is made, in the kitchen and in the shop. It gives up automatic detection, which depends on receipts or sensors that are hard to get right. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of items in the use-first list that are eaten before they spoil, from a quick self-report tap, and where a shop partner exists, the reduction in items bought and thrown. Input: the share of weeks in which the household checks the list before shopping. Guardrails: nudges per week, and opt-outs. Counter metric: food-safety incidents reported, and the share of people who say the app made them feel guilty. <em>Risks.</em> Safety is the main one, and it is specific to this product: a feature that pushes people to eat food that is spoiled can harm them. The design response is to treat &quot;use by&quot; dates as safety limits and &quot;best before&quot; dates as quality guides, to be conservative in the wording, and never to tell a user that a food is safe; when in doubt it says to throw it away. Wellbeing: no guilt, no shaming counters. Privacy: receipts and shopping lists reveal habits and health, so they stay on the device by default. I'd change my mind if the first tests showed that the problem was not forgetting but over-buying by habit in the shop, which would move the product from the fridge to the shopping list.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you improve the inbox of a widely used email app for people who receive more than 100 emails a day?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists features (smarter search, more labels, a dark mode) without saying who the user is or where the inbox fails them.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States what the product is for, chooses a segment and a step, names a specific problem, offers different types of move and recommends one.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats a wrong automatic decision as the main risk, protects people who rely on the current view, sizes the gain and sets a guardrail on missed important mail.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume the existing app on phones and the web, and a goal of making the inbox the place where heavy email users get their work done. I'll note that I know the product only from public information and use. <em>State.</em> Role: a utility that anchors the user to the company's wider account and services. Users: people with high volume (managers, sales, support, executives), moderate users, and casual users. Funnel: mail arrives, the person triages (decides what needs action), reads, acts, then files or deletes. Metrics I'd expect it to follow: daily use, time in the app, and search use. Constraints: years of mail in people's folders and labels, habits around a chronological view, and the same behavior across devices. <em>Area.</em> I choose people receiving 100 or more emails a day at the triage step. Their reach is smaller than the whole base, perhaps 5% of users (invented), but the pain is highest, the effort repeats every day and they influence which tool their teams use. I give up casual users, who have little to improve. <em>Problem.</em> A heavy user cannot tell quickly which messages need a reply from them and which only inform. The workarounds are the evidence: starring, moving mail into folders by hand, and rereading the inbox several times a day. I'd verify by watching three people triage and by looking at how many messages people open and then leave unanswered. <em>The four moves.</em> Fix: make the existing importance signals more accurate and more visible, since wrong ones teach users to ignore them. Add: a &quot;needs reply&quot; section built from signals such as being addressed directly, a question in the text and the person's past replies to that sender. Remove: take out manual steps in triage, such as having to label a thread to keep track of it. Reposition: treat the inbox as a task list. <em>Size.</em> If 5% of 500,000,000 users (invented) are heavy, that is 25,000,000 people. If triage takes 40 minutes a day and the section saves 10% of it, that is 4 minutes a day per person; with a 30% haircut for people who ignore the section, about 2.8 minutes, so about 70,000,000 minutes a day across the group, or about 1.2 million hours a day. The number is large because of scale, and I'd still check it with a test, since it assumes people trust the section. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend the Add, with the Fix, in the first version: a &quot;needs reply&quot; section above the normal inbox, never replacing it, with the reason shown (&quot;addressed to you, contains a question&quot;). It addresses the triage problem by giving up a more ambitious reposition. Messages are only highlighted, never hidden, because a wrong decision that buries an important email is the worst failure and the one that destroys trust. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of messages in the section that the user replies to within 24 hours. Input: the share of heavy users who open the section daily. Guardrail: the share of replied-to messages that the section had not flagged, which measures what it misses, and it should stay under 5% (invented). Counter metric: the share of flagged messages that the user dismisses without action, which measures noise. <em>Risks.</em> Legacy users who rely on the chronological view keep it as the default, with the section optional. Misjudged importance could favor some senders, so the signals are shown and explained, and the user can turn them off. I'd change my mind if watching users showed that triage was fast already and the cost was in writing replies, in which case the lever is drafting help and not sorting. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that improvement prompts at Google can concern its own products at large scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Improve the order-tracking screen of a food-delivery app.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a map and push notifications,&quot; with no reasoning about the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the customer's problem while waiting, chooses a fix over an addition where it is better, sizes it and gives a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that an honest estimate can hurt conversion at checkout, names the second-order effect and keeps a counter metric on it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The product is a marketplace and its role is revenue and retention: customers who get food when they expect it come back. The segment is customers who have ordered and are waiting. The step is from &quot;order placed&quot; to &quot;delivered,&quot; and the pain is uncertainty: they do not know when the food will arrive or why it is late. The evidence I'd look for is contacts to support asking &quot;where is my order?&quot;, which I'd assume at 6 per 100 orders (invented), and the cost of each contact, assumed at $3 (invented). <em>Moves.</em> Fix: make the estimated time accurate, and show a range with a reason when something changes (&quot;the restaurant is taking longer&quot;). Add: a live map of the courier. Remove: take away the minute-by-minute countdown that shows precision the app does not have; a countdown that is wrong damages trust more than a range. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix with the Remove: honest, accurate estimates, which cost less than a map and address the cause of the contacts. <em>Size.</em> With 1,000,000 orders a month (invented), 6% contact support: 60,000. If the change cuts contacts by 25%, that is 15,000 fewer; with a 30% haircut, 10,500, or about $31,500 a month in support cost. The cost saving is modest, and the larger effect is likely on satisfaction and reordering, which I'd measure and not assume. <em>Second-order effect.</em> An honest range is longer than an optimistic number, and the estimate shown at checkout affects whether a customer orders at all. If I show &quot;35 to 45 minutes&quot; where the app said &quot;30,&quot; conversion could fall. So the checkout estimate and the tracking estimate must come from the same model, and I'd test checkout conversion as a guardrail. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: support contacts about delivery time per 100 orders. Input: the share of orders delivered within the shown range. Guardrail: conversion at checkout. Counter metric: the share of customers who cancel after seeing a longer estimate, which shows whether honesty is driving them away. I'd change my mind if the data showed that most contacts were about missing items rather than timing, in which case the lever is order accuracy.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Improve a notes app that syncs across phone, tablet and laptop.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add tags, a better editor and handwriting support,&quot; with no reasoning about the cross-device experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Focuses on the cross-device problem, names legacy users and consistency as constraints, and chooses a fix over a feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats trust as the product, chooses the metric that matches the failure (lost content, not engagement), and handles encryption, older versions and offline editing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume the notes app that comes with the user's devices, and I know it only from public information and use. Role: a utility that makes each device more useful and keeps users within the same set of devices; its value grows with every device a person adds, so cross-device behavior is central. Users: heavy note-takers (students and professionals with thousands of notes), casual users (lists and reminders) and people who share notes. Funnel: capture, organize, find, revisit on another device, share. Constraints: years of notes and folder structures, consistent behavior across every device, offline editing, encrypted notes, and devices running older versions. <em>Area.</em> I choose heavy note-takers at the moment of opening a note on a second device. Their stakes are the highest because their notes are their work, and the step has a specific failure: a note edited on one device appears stale or differs on another, or two edits clash and one is lost. I give up casual users, who rarely feel it. I'd verify by looking at how often a note changes on two devices within a short time and what users do after a conflict. <em>Problem.</em> &quot;I edited a note on my phone, opened it on my laptop and saw the old version, or lost a paragraph.&quot; The workarounds are the evidence: copying text into a message to myself, saving a second copy, or emailing notes to myself. These show a loss of trust, which for a notes app is the heart of the product. <em>Moves.</em> Fix: show a clear sync state (&quot;up to date&quot; or &quot;syncing&quot;) and handle a conflict by keeping both versions and not by overwriting. Add: a version history, so any loss can be undone. Remove: nothing. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix and a small Add: visible sync state, a keep-both rule for conflicts, and a short version history. <em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: existing notes and folders must be untouched, and no change to how a note is stored should require action. Ecosystem: the behavior must work the same on every device, so I'd stage the rollout across all of them, and on devices on older versions the new behavior degrades gracefully, with no loss. Privacy: for encrypted notes the conflict is resolved on the devices, and the service never needs to read the note. <em>Size.</em> Assume 20,000,000 people use two or more devices (invented). If 4% hit a conflict or a stale view in a month, that is 800,000; if 10% of those lose content, 80,000. A keep-both rule that prevents 80% of losses prevents 64,000 losses a month. The count is small against the base, and the severity is high: one lost note can end a person's use of the app. So the size does not say &quot;skip it&quot;; it says to measure the right thing. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: lost-content incidents per 100,000 multi-device users per month, from reports and detected overwrites, with a target near zero. Input: the share of conflicts resolved by keeping both versions. Guardrails: sync delay at the 95th percentile, and storage used by duplicate versions. Counter metric: complaints about duplicate notes, since keep-both can clutter a library. I'd change my mind if the data showed that conflicts were rare and the common complaint was finding notes, in which case the area is search. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that improvement prompts at Apple concern its own devices and weigh consistency across them <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about how any existing notes product works.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Improve the walking experience in a maps app.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add augmented-reality directions and more landmarks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments walkers, chooses a moment in the walking journey, names the problem, offers different moves and recommends one with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the hardware (a watch, haptics) to keep the phone in a pocket, addresses distracted walking and battery, and protects users who rely on audio or who drive.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume the built-in maps app on phones and watches. Role: a utility that makes the devices more useful, with walking as one of several modes alongside driving and transit. Users: commuters walking to transit, visitors in an unfamiliar area, and errand walkers. I'll organize the walking journey as plan, start, move, arrive. | Step | Reach | Pain or loss | Headroom | Choice | |---|---|---|---|---| | Plan the route | High | Low | Low | Rejected | | Start walking: which way do I go? | High | Medium to high: the person does not know which direction to walk | High | Chosen | | Move: follow the turns | High | Medium | Medium | Second | | Arrive: find the right entrance | Medium | Medium | Medium | Rejected for now | <em>Problem.</em> At the start of a walk, the person standing on a street corner cannot tell which way to go. The map dot may not show the direction they face, so they walk a few steps, watch the dot, and correct. The workaround is the evidence (walking the wrong way for half a minute). Visitors in an unfamiliar area feel it most, since they have no sense of direction to lean on. <em>Moves.</em> Fix: improve the accuracy of the facing direction and give a plain first instruction (&quot;walk toward the park, on Oak Street&quot;). Add: turn cues on the watch, a tap for left and a different tap for right, so the person need not look at the phone, which also helps in the rain and in a crowd. Remove: reduce the map's detail while walking, since clutter makes the route harder to see. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix with the watch cues. They use what the devices are good at (sensing direction, haptics on the wrist) and they address the start. <em>Size.</em> Assume 50,000,000 navigation sessions a week (invented), 8% on foot: 4,000,000. If 15% begin in the wrong direction, that is 600,000; the change cuts it by 40%: 240,000; with a 30% haircut, 168,000 avoided wrong starts. At 1.5 minutes saved each, that is 252,000 minutes, or about 4,200 hours a week. The gain is in time and confidence, and I'd say the value is the experience and not a number tied to revenue. <em>Constraints and risks.</em> Legacy users: drivers and transit riders must see no change. Safety: the point of the haptic cues is to reduce screen-watching while walking, and I'd test that screen-on time falls. Accessibility: people who use audio guidance should get the first instruction spoken as well. Battery: continuous direction sensing costs power, so it runs only while navigating. Privacy: the facing direction is computed on the device. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of walking routes whose first 30 seconds follow the route direction, inferred from location. Input: the share of walkers using watch cues. Guardrails: battery use per walking hour, and screen-on time while walking, which should fall. Counter metric: the share of users who turn the cues off, since cues that annoy will be disabled. I'd change my mind if the data showed that wrong starts were rare and the real loss was at the arrival (the wrong entrance), in which case I'd move to that step. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that improvement prompts at Apple concern its devices and the experience across them <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about how any existing maps product works.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-05</h3>\n";
